<%# Consent banner for the measurement tags. Rendered only where those tags are
    allowed to run, so a visitor never sees it on a page that has nothing to
    consent to.

    It starts hidden and is revealed by the analytics controller, which is the
    only thing that knows whether this browser already answered. Rendering it
    visible and hiding it in JS would flash the banner on every page for people
    who decided months ago.

    The policy link opens in a new tab on purpose: this banner is waiting on a
    decision, and navigating away from it would lose the decision. %>
<div class="cookie-consent shadow-lg" data-analytics-target="banner" hidden
     role="region" aria-label="<%= t("cookie_consent.aria_label") %>">
  <div class="cookie-consent__text">
    <strong><%= t("cookie_consent.title") %></strong>
    <p class="mb-0 small">
      <%= t("cookie_consent.body") %>
      <%= link_to t("cookie_consent.more"), data_policy_path, target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="cookie-consent__actions">
    <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm"
            data-action="analytics#reject">
      <%= t("cookie_consent.reject") %>
    </button>
    <button type="button" class="btn btn-success btn-sm"
            data-action="analytics#accept">
      <%= t("cookie_consent.accept") %>
    </button>
  </div>
</div>
